use std
into doe.amni
dialect js

This file is a library built for the web as a wasm module; its `main()` does nothing. JavaScript drives it through its exported functions (every name starting with doe_ is exported). It lays out design-of-experiments sample points in coded units from -1 to 1, fits a polynomial response surface to measured results by least squares, and searches that surface for its best point. Its only import line is `import math;`, ending with a semicolon. Every int used in real math is converted with `as real`, and a real used as an int with `as int`, since Helium has no real or int conversion calls.

```amni
extern fn doe_view_f64(id i32, xs real[:]) = "doe_view_f64";
```

Module state. Ints `NP` (point count) and `NK` (variable count), both starting at 0, and int `SEED` starting at 7. Growable f64 arrays: `IN` (16 inputs), `OUT` (64 outputs), `X` (NP times NK coded coordinates, row by row), `Y` (NP measured responses), `B` (fitted coefficients), `PRED` (NP fitted values), `EFF` (NK signed main effects), `XO` (NK coordinates of the best point), `A` (normal matrix), `R` (right-hand side), `F` (one row of terms) and `T` (scratch, NP times NK).

Sizing helper. `zf(xs, n, x)` takes an f64 growable array by reference: xs is emptied, then gets `reserve`(n), then is resized to n entries equal to x. On the web an array that holds entries cannot grow past the room it first reserved, so every array is emptied before it is sized again.

Random helper. `rnd()` returns a real in [0, 1): SEED becomes SEED times 1103515245 plus 12345, kept as SEED modulo 2147483648 (adding 2147483648 first if it is negative), and the result is SEED as real divided by 2147483648.0.

The exported function `doe_views()`, declared with the export keyword with i32 parameters (each converted with `as int` at the top) and an i32 result, registers the arrays with `doe_view_f64`: 1 IN, 2 OUT, 3 X, 4 Y, 5 B, 7 PRED, 8 EFF, 9 XO. It returns 0.

The exported function `doe_init()`, declared with the export keyword with i32 parameters (each converted with `as int` at the top) and an i32 result, sizes IN to 16 zeros and OUT to 64 zeros with `zf`, calls `doe_views()` and returns 0.

The exported function `doe_size(np, nk)`, declared with the export keyword with i32 parameters (each converted with `as int` at the top) and an i32 result, sets NP to np and NK to nk. With p equal to 1 plus 2 times nk plus nk times (nk minus 1) divided by 2, it sizes with `zf` to zeros: X to np times nk (at least 1), T to np times nk (at least 1), Y to np (at least 1), PRED to np (at least 1), EFF to nk (at least 1), XO to nk (at least 1), B to p, R to p, F to p and A to p times p. It calls `doe_views()` and returns 0.

The exported function `doe_lhs(n, k, seed, tries)`, declared with the export keyword with i32 parameters (each converted with `as int` at the top) and an i32 result, fills X with a Latin hypercube of n points in k variables, keeping the most spread-out of `tries` candidates (at least 1). It calls `doe_size(n, k)` and sets SEED to seed. best starts at -1.0. For each try: for each variable j below k, it fills a permutation: T[i times k plus j] becomes i as real for i below n, then for i from n minus 1 down to 1 it picks r as (`rnd()` times (i plus 1) as real) as int clamped to at most i and swaps T[i times k plus j] with T[r times k plus j]; then each entry T[i times k plus j] becomes ((T[i times k plus j] plus `rnd()`) divided by n as real) times 2.0 minus 1.0. It then finds dmin, the smallest squared distance between any two different points (rows) of T, starting from 1e300. If dmin is above best, best becomes dmin and X is copied from T for all n times k entries. It sets OUT[0] to best and returns n.

The exported function `doe_ff(levels, k)`, declared with the export keyword with i32 parameters (each converted with `as int` at the top) and an i32 result, fills X with the full factorial of `levels` evenly spaced values from -1 to 1 in each of k variables (levels is at least 2). n is levels raised to the power k, built by multiplying. It calls `doe_size(n, k)`. For each point i below n: c starts at i; for each variable j below k, d is c modulo levels, X[i times k plus j] becomes -1.0 plus 2.0 times d as real divided by (levels minus 1) as real, and c becomes c divided by levels. It returns n.

The exported function `doe_ccd(k)`, declared with the export keyword with i32 parameters (each converted with `as int` at the top) and an i32 result, fills X with a face-centered central composite design in k variables: 2 to the power k corner points, then 2 times k axial points, then one center point. n is the corner count plus 2 times k plus 1. It calls `doe_size(n, k)`. Corner point i (below the corner count) has X[i times k plus j] equal to 1.0 when bit j of i is set and -1.0 otherwise. Axial point a (below 2 times k) at row corner count plus a has every coordinate 0.0 except variable a divided by 2, which is -1.0 when a is even and 1.0 when a is odd. The last row is all 0.0. It returns n.

Term helper. `terms(xs, off, k, mode)` fills F with the model terms of the point whose coordinates are xs[off] to xs[off plus k minus 1] and returns how many terms it wrote. F[0] is 1.0 and the count starts at 1. When mode is at least 1, the k coordinates follow. When mode is 3, the k squares follow. When mode is at least 2, the products xs[off plus i] times xs[off plus j] for every i below j follow, i outer and j inner.

Prediction helper. `pv(xs, off, k, mode, p)` calls `terms(xs, off, k, mode)` and returns the sum over t below p of B[t] times F[t].

Count helper. `nterm(k, mode)` returns 1, plus k when mode is at least 1, plus k when mode is 3, plus k times (k minus 1) divided by 2 when mode is at least 2.

The exported function `doe_fit(n, k, want)`, declared with the export keyword with i32 parameters (each converted with `as int` at the top) and an i32 result, fits Y over the n points of X. The mode is want (0 constant, 1 linear, 2 linear with interactions, 3 full quadratic), lowered by one step at a time while `nterm(k, mode)` is above n and mode is above 0. With p equal to `nterm(k, mode)`, it clears A (p times p) and R (p), then for each point i adds F[a] times F[b] to A[a times p plus b] for all a and b below p and F[a] times Y[i] to R[a], after `terms(X, i times k, k, mode)`. It adds 1e-9 times (1.0 plus A[a times p plus a]) to each diagonal entry. It solves A times B equals R with a Cholesky factorization done in place in A (lower triangle: for each column j, the diagonal becomes the square root of the diagonal minus the sum of squares of A[j times p plus m] for m below j, floored at 1e-300; each entry below it in row i becomes (A[i times p plus j] minus the sum over m below j of A[i times p plus m] times A[j times p plus m]) divided by the diagonal), then a forward solve into B and a backward solve in place. It sets PRED[i] to `pv(X, i times k, k, mode, p)` for every point. With ym the mean of Y, sse the sum of squares of Y[i] minus PRED[i] and sst the sum of squares of Y[i] minus ym: OUT[0] is p as real, OUT[1] is 1.0 minus sse divided by sst when sst is above 1e-300 times (1.0 plus ym times ym) and 1.0 otherwise, OUT[2] is the square root of sse divided by n as real, and OUT[3] is mode as real. When mode is at least 1, EFF[j] is 2.0 times B[1 plus j] for each variable j, else 0.0. It returns p.

The exported function `doe_best(n, k, mode, sense)`, declared with the export keyword with i32 parameters (each converted with `as int` at the top) and an i32 result, searches the fitted surface (with p equal to `nterm(k, mode)`) inside the cube from -1 to 1 for its largest value when sense is above 0 and its smallest otherwise. s is 1.0 when sense is above 0 and -1.0 otherwise. It starts XO at the measured point i whose s times Y[i] is largest (copying its k coordinates). Then for 7 sweeps, for each variable j, it tries the 41 values -1.0 plus 0.05 times m for m from 0 to 40 in XO[j], keeping the one whose s times `pv(XO, 0, k, mode, p)` is largest. It sets OUT[4] to `pv(XO, 0, k, mode, p)` and returns 0.

`main()` does nothing.
